1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,070
June 1962.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:04,310
Atlanta Federal Penitentiary.

3
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:07,870
A man in prison grays picks
up a length of iron pipe.

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:09,970
He has not slept properly in weeks.

5
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:13,850
He is sure that somewhere in this yard,
someone has been sent to kill him.

6
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,930
And he has just recognized the face.

7
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:16,990
He swings.

8
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:19,290
The man on the ground is a stranger.

9
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:20,550
Not a hit man.

10
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:21,710
Not a mobster.

11
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,290
Just the wrong face, in the wrong place.

12
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:26,150
The killer's name is Joe Valachi.

13
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:30,450
Thirty years a soldier in one of the
most powerful crime families in America.

14
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:33,110
And in that moment, he
has nowhere left to go.

15
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:34,930
His own boss wants him dead.

16
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:36,930
The government wants him for murder.

17
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:39,870
So he does the one thing
no made man had ever done.

18
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:40,850
He talks.

19
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,210
Fifteen months later,

20
00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:44,770
under the guard of two
hundred federal marshals,

21
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:47,990
on live television, in front
of the United States Senate,

22
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,210
he will say the words his bosses
swore would never be spoken.

23
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:52,370
Cosa Nostra.

24
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:52,750
Our thing.

25
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,110
This is Black Dossier.

26
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:55,530
And this...

27
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:57,140
is the Valachi file.

28
00:00:59,814 --> 00:01:01,122
Chapter One. A Boy From East Harlem.

29
00:01:01,172 --> 00:01:06,102
Joseph Valachi was born in
1904, in East Harlem, New York.

30
00:01:06,152 --> 00:01:10,062
His parents were poor immigrants from
the Campania region of southern Italy.

31
00:01:10,112 --> 00:01:12,602
Years later, he would
describe a hard childhood,

32
00:01:12,652 --> 00:01:15,002
and a violent, hard-drinking father.

33
00:01:15,052 --> 00:01:16,902
He did not start in the Mafia.

34
00:01:16,952 --> 00:01:18,242
He started small.

35
00:01:18,292 --> 00:01:21,802
As a young man he drove for a gang
of burglars called the Minutemen.

36
00:01:21,852 --> 00:01:24,762
Their method was simple:
smash a store window,

37
00:01:24,812 --> 00:01:26,562
grab what they could, and disappear.

38
00:01:26,612 --> 00:01:28,782
Valachi was the getaway driver.

39
00:01:28,832 --> 00:01:30,822
He was arrested more than once.

40
00:01:30,872 --> 00:01:36,002
In 1923, after a robbery went wrong,
he was sent to Sing Sing prison.

41
00:01:36,052 --> 00:01:39,262
Sing Sing sat on the banks of the
Hudson River, north of the city.

42
00:01:39,312 --> 00:01:42,102
It was one of the most
notorious prisons in America.

43
00:01:42,152 --> 00:01:45,452
For a young street thief from
Harlem, it was also a school.

44
00:01:45,502 --> 00:01:48,102
Valachi served only part of his sentence,

45
00:01:48,152 --> 00:01:50,542
about nine months, before he was released.

46
00:01:50,592 --> 00:01:53,042
He came out with a record, a reputation,

47
00:01:53,092 --> 00:01:55,802
and a growing list of
people who knew his name.

48
00:01:55,852 --> 00:01:57,542
Prison did not change his direction.

49
00:01:57,592 --> 00:01:58,882
It sharpened it.

50
00:01:58,932 --> 00:02:01,732
Inside, and back out on
the streets of East Harlem,

51
00:02:01,782 --> 00:02:04,162
Valachi learned who really ran things.

52
00:02:04,212 --> 00:02:05,542
Not the street gangs.

53
00:02:05,592 --> 00:02:06,922
Not the burglars.

54
00:02:06,972 --> 00:02:08,902
Above them, there were older men.

55
00:02:08,952 --> 00:02:09,882
Quieter men.

56
00:02:09,932 --> 00:02:13,642
Men who did not need to break windows,
because other people paid them.

57
00:02:13,692 --> 00:02:15,962
In New York at the end of the 1920s,

58
00:02:16,012 --> 00:02:18,202
those men were about to
go to war with each other.

59
00:02:18,252 --> 00:02:20,812
And Joe Valachi was about to pick a side.

60
00:02:22,745 --> 00:02:24,676
Chapter Two. The War Of 1930.

61
00:02:24,726 --> 00:02:29,636
By 1930, the Italian underworld of New
York was divided between two powerful

62
00:02:29,686 --> 00:02:30,656
bosses.

63
00:02:30,706 --> 00:02:32,949
On one side, Joe Masseria.

64
00:02:32,999 --> 00:02:36,416
Old-fashioned, heavy-handed,
and used to being obeyed.

65
00:02:36,466 --> 00:02:38,366
On the other, Salvatore Maranzano.

66
00:02:38,416 --> 00:02:42,276
A newcomer from Castellammare
del Golfo, in Sicily.

67
00:02:42,326 --> 00:02:46,436
The same coastal town that produced
so many of the men in our first file.

68
00:02:46,486 --> 00:02:48,956
Historians call what followed
the Castellammarese War.

69
00:02:49,006 --> 00:02:50,816
For more than a year,

70
00:02:50,866 --> 00:02:55,136
men from both sides were ambushed in
restaurants, in garages, on the street.

71
00:02:55,186 --> 00:02:58,956
Valachi did not start on
Maranzano's side by accident.

72
00:02:59,006 --> 00:03:01,716
His first connection was to
the crew of Gaetano Reina,

73
00:03:01,766 --> 00:03:05,816
a boss who controlled ice distribution
in the Bronx, among other rackets.

74
00:03:05,866 --> 00:03:10,276
When Reina was shot dead in early
1930, his men were pushed into the war.

75
00:03:10,326 --> 00:03:14,716
Two years later, Valachi would marry
Reina's eldest daughter, Carmela.

76
00:03:14,766 --> 00:03:16,716
Loyalty in that world was personal.

77
00:03:16,766 --> 00:03:18,006
It was also temporary.

78
00:03:18,056 --> 00:03:20,696
Valachi fought on Maranzano's side.

79
00:03:20,746 --> 00:03:23,016
And that same year, 1930,

80
00:03:23,066 --> 00:03:24,756
he was brought to a private house,

81
00:03:24,806 --> 00:03:26,676
for a ceremony he would describe,

82
00:03:26,726 --> 00:03:29,796
more than three decades
later, in remarkable detail.

83
00:03:29,846 --> 00:03:31,936
He said around forty men were there.

84
00:03:31,986 --> 00:03:36,036
On a table in front of him lay a
gun, a thirty-eight, and a knife.

85
00:03:36,086 --> 00:03:37,496
Maranzano presided.

86
00:03:37,546 --> 00:03:41,236
Another man pricked Valachi's finger
with a pin and squeezed until the blood

87
00:03:41,286 --> 00:03:42,096
came out.

88
00:03:42,146 --> 00:03:44,476
The man holding the pin was Joe Bonanno.

89
00:03:44,526 --> 00:03:45,856
Remember that name.

90
00:03:45,906 --> 00:03:46,956
Then Maranzano spoke.

91
00:03:47,006 --> 00:03:51,536
According to Valachi, he said the
blood meant they were now one family.

92
00:03:51,586 --> 00:03:54,376
And he warned them: you live
by the gun and the knife,

93
00:03:54,426 --> 00:03:56,226
and you die by the gun and the knife.

94
00:03:56,276 --> 00:03:58,376
Valachi was now a made man.

95
00:03:58,426 --> 00:04:00,936
The war ended in April 1931,

96
00:04:00,986 --> 00:04:04,876
when Joe Masseria was shot to death
in a restaurant in Coney Island.

97
00:04:04,926 --> 00:04:07,646
Maranzano declared himself
the boss of all bosses.

98
00:04:07,696 --> 00:04:10,216
Valachi became one of his bodyguards.

99
00:04:10,266 --> 00:04:12,096
It lasted about five months.

100
00:04:12,146 --> 00:04:16,056
In September 1931, Maranzano
was murdered in his own office.

101
00:04:16,106 --> 00:04:18,696
The men behind it, by most accounts,

102
00:04:18,746 --> 00:04:22,396
answered to an ambitious young
gangster named Charles Luciano.

103
00:04:22,446 --> 00:04:23,406
Lucky Luciano.

104
00:04:23,456 --> 00:04:26,416
Valachi switched sides, as many did.

105
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:29,896
He was now a soldier in the family that
would one day carry the name of a man

106
00:04:29,946 --> 00:04:32,596
he would come to fear
more than anyone alive.

107
00:04:32,646 --> 00:04:33,746
Vito Genovese.

108
00:04:35,734 --> 00:04:36,950
Chapter Three. Thirty Years A Soldier.

109
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,010
For the next three decades,
Joe Valachi was not a boss.

110
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:42,290
He was not a star.

111
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:43,490
He was a soldier.

112
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:47,510
He worked in a crew run by Anthony
Strollo, known as Tony Bender.

113
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:49,750
He ran slot machines and the numbers,

114
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:53,170
an illegal neighborhood lottery that
brought in steady cash a few cents at a

115
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:53,930
time.

116
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:55,950
He owned pieces of small businesses.

117
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:57,070
He loaned money.

118
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,070
At times, he dealt in narcotics.

119
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:01,990
And when the family asked,
he did other things.

120
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:05,510
Investigators would later suspect him
of involvement in many killings over the

121
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:06,370
years.

122
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:08,210
Years later, before the Senate,

123
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:11,710
he summed up what he did for the
organization in a few cold words:

124
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,430
I just go out and kill for them.

125
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:14,630
That is important.

126
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:17,610
The man about to expose
the Mafia was not a hero.

127
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:20,370
He was not a victim who
stumbled into the wrong crowd.

128
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:21,570
He was one of them.

129
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:24,570
But he was also something the
bosses had never worried about:

130
00:05:24,620 --> 00:05:27,750
a low-ranking man who had been in
the room for almost everything.

131
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:28,650
The war.

132
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:29,790
The initiation.

133
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,270
The rise of Luciano.

134
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:34,410
The quiet takeovers of
rackets across New York.

135
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:37,150
He had watched the organization
grow for thirty years.

136
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:38,330
And he remembered.

137
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:42,330
One case from those years shows
exactly how close he stood to the top.

138
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:45,450
In 1953, according to later accounts,

139
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:48,540
Vito Genovese wanted a man
named Steven Franse dead.

140
00:05:48,590 --> 00:05:51,110
Franse had been close to Genovese's wife,

141
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:53,970
Anna, who had left Genovese
and taken him to court,

142
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:56,350
airing details of his business in public.

143
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:57,970
Genovese blamed Franse.

144
00:05:58,020 --> 00:06:00,650
On June 18th, 1953,

145
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:03,910
Valachi invited Franse to a
restaurant he owned in the Bronx.

146
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:05,450
Two other men were waiting inside.

147
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:07,430
Franse never left.

148
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:09,950
Valachi would later
describe the murder himself.

149
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,630
It was the kind of work a loyal
soldier did without asking questions.

150
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:16,570
And it was exactly the kind of secret
that could get a soldier killed,

151
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:18,980
once his boss stopped trusting him.

152
00:06:19,758 --> 00:06:22,368
Chapter Four. The Kiss.

153
00:06:22,418 --> 00:06:27,968
In 1959, Valachi was convicted on federal
narcotics charges and sentenced to

154
00:06:28,018 --> 00:06:29,268
fifteen years.

155
00:06:29,318 --> 00:06:33,388
That same year, the man at the top
of his family was convicted too.

156
00:06:33,438 --> 00:06:36,768
Vito Genovese, the boss at
the center of our first file,

157
00:06:36,818 --> 00:06:40,008
received fifteen years in
a federal narcotics case.

158
00:06:40,058 --> 00:06:44,508
They ended up in the same place: the
federal penitentiary in Atlanta, Georgia.

159
00:06:44,558 --> 00:06:47,568
It is hard to imagine a worse
place for a nervous man.

160
00:06:47,618 --> 00:06:49,968
The Atlanta penitentiary
held hundreds of inmates,

161
00:06:50,018 --> 00:06:53,608
and among them were men who owed
favors to the Genovese family,

162
00:06:53,658 --> 00:06:56,068
and men who would do anything to earn one.

163
00:06:56,118 --> 00:06:59,378
For a boss like Genovese,
prison was not the end of power.

164
00:06:59,428 --> 00:07:02,388
Messages went in and out
with visitors and lawyers.

165
00:07:02,438 --> 00:07:03,928
Orders could still be given.

166
00:07:03,978 --> 00:07:06,448
And inside the walls, he
had people around him.

167
00:07:06,498 --> 00:07:08,568
And Genovese, according to Valachi,

168
00:07:08,618 --> 00:07:11,988
had come to suspect that Valachi
was talking to the government.

169
00:07:12,038 --> 00:07:15,108
Valachi later described a
conversation in his cell.

170
00:07:15,158 --> 00:07:18,988
Genovese spoke about a rotten apple
that has to be removed from the barrel.

171
00:07:19,038 --> 00:07:21,848
And then, Genovese
kissed him on the cheek.

172
00:07:21,898 --> 00:07:24,028
Valachi understood it as a kiss of death.

173
00:07:24,078 --> 00:07:26,088
He became afraid of the prison food.

174
00:07:26,138 --> 00:07:28,108
He stayed close to the walls.

175
00:07:28,158 --> 00:07:30,068
He watched every face in the yard.

176
00:07:30,118 --> 00:07:32,588
He became convinced that
a particular inmate,

177
00:07:32,638 --> 00:07:36,388
a mobster named Joseph DiPalermo,
had been sent to kill him.

178
00:07:36,438 --> 00:07:39,128
Outside, there were reasons to be afraid.

179
00:07:39,178 --> 00:07:42,288
That April, Valachi's
longtime boss, Tony Bender,

180
00:07:42,338 --> 00:07:46,048
had walked out of his home in New
Jersey and was never seen again.

181
00:07:46,098 --> 00:07:48,628
In that world, everyone
knew what that meant.

182
00:07:48,678 --> 00:07:52,648
If Tony Bender could disappear, so
could a soldier in a prison yard.

183
00:07:52,698 --> 00:07:55,028
On a day in June 1962,

184
00:07:55,078 --> 00:07:57,488
near a construction
area in the prison yard,

185
00:07:57,538 --> 00:07:59,628
Valachi picked up a length of iron pipe.

186
00:07:59,678 --> 00:08:01,388
He attacked a man from behind.

187
00:08:01,438 --> 00:08:03,488
The man was not Joseph DiPalermo.

188
00:08:03,538 --> 00:08:07,308
He was John Saupp, an inmate with
no connection to the Mafia at all.

189
00:08:07,358 --> 00:08:09,488
Saupp died a few days later.

190
00:08:09,538 --> 00:08:11,828
Joe Valachi had killed the wrong man.

191
00:08:11,878 --> 00:08:13,828
Now he was facing a murder charge,

192
00:08:13,878 --> 00:08:18,068
a death sentence from his own family,
and the rest of his life behind bars.

193
00:08:18,118 --> 00:08:19,558
He had one move left.

194
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,650
Chapter Five. The Deal.

195
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:25,230
Valachi reached out to
the federal government.

196
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:27,550
Over the following months, he was moved,

197
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,770
isolated, and questioned at
length by federal agents.

198
00:08:30,820 --> 00:08:32,470
He was held under heavy guard,

199
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:36,190
because if the Genovese family
could reach him, he was a dead man.

200
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:38,450
He was eventually brought
to Washington, D.C.,

201
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:41,930
and kept in a jail cell, away
from every other prisoner.

202
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:44,610
A Justice Department
lawyer, William Hundley,

203
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:46,820
took responsibility for keeping him alive.

204
00:08:46,870 --> 00:08:50,930
Agents sat with him day
after day, filling notebooks.

205
00:08:50,980 --> 00:08:53,526
Valachi claimed that Genovese
had put a price on his head:

206
00:08:53,576 --> 00:08:56,590
one hundred thousand dollars
to whoever silenced him.

207
00:08:56,640 --> 00:09:00,550
He was sentenced to life in prison
for the killing in Atlanta, not death.

208
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:01,930
And he began to talk.

209
00:09:01,980 --> 00:09:04,190
He talked about the war of 1930.

210
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:07,190
He talked about the ceremony
with the gun and the knife.

211
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:09,110
He talked about who reported to whom.

212
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:10,990
And he gave the government a name.

213
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:15,310
For decades, investigators and
journalists had used the word Mafia.

214
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:18,410
Valachi said the members themselves
called it something else.

215
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:19,490
Cosa Nostra.

216
00:09:19,540 --> 00:09:20,310
Our thing.

217
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:23,570
To the Department of Justice,
this was extraordinary.

218
00:09:23,620 --> 00:09:26,410
The attorney general of the
United States, Robert F.

219
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:30,290
Kennedy, the same man we met in our first
file working for a Senate committee,

220
00:09:30,340 --> 00:09:33,050
was now running the
war on organized crime.

221
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:36,490
He would call Valachi's information
the biggest single intelligence

222
00:09:36,540 --> 00:09:40,290
breakthrough yet in combating organized
crime and racketeering in the United

223
00:09:40,340 --> 00:09:41,250
States.

224
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:44,070
But information inside a
government file can be denied.

225
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:46,430
Information on television cannot.

226
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:50,010
So the government made a decision
that no one had ever made before.

227
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:52,300
They would put a made man
in front of the cameras.

228
00:09:53,235 --> 00:09:55,765
Chapter Six. Live From The Senate.

229
00:09:55,815 --> 00:09:59,025
On September 25th, 1963,

230
00:09:59,075 --> 00:10:02,045
the Senate's Permanent
Subcommittee on Investigations,

231
00:10:02,095 --> 00:10:06,545
led by Senator John McClellan, opened
its hearings on organized crime.

232
00:10:06,595 --> 00:10:08,625
The first witness was not Valachi.

233
00:10:08,675 --> 00:10:11,285
It was the attorney
general, Robert Kennedy.

234
00:10:11,335 --> 00:10:13,965
McClellan set the tone
in his opening statement.

235
00:10:14,015 --> 00:10:17,405
The existence of such a criminal
organization as Cosa Nostra,

236
00:10:17,455 --> 00:10:19,145
he said, is frightening.

237
00:10:19,195 --> 00:10:21,045
Two days later, Valachi arrived.

238
00:10:21,095 --> 00:10:23,785
He sat at the witness
table in a plain suit,

239
00:10:23,835 --> 00:10:27,625
wearing glasses, speaking in the rough
New York accent of the streets he grew

240
00:10:27,675 --> 00:10:28,725
up on.

241
00:10:28,775 --> 00:10:30,505
Over several days of testimony,

242
00:10:30,555 --> 00:10:32,645
he walked the senators, and the country,

243
00:10:32,695 --> 00:10:34,885
through the organization from the inside.

244
00:10:34,935 --> 00:10:36,085
There were families.

245
00:10:36,135 --> 00:10:37,905
Each family had a boss.

246
00:10:37,955 --> 00:10:40,685
Below the boss were
lieutenants, the caporegimes.

247
00:10:40,735 --> 00:10:42,665
Below them, the soldiers.

248
00:10:42,715 --> 00:10:46,345
Above all the families sat a commission
of bosses who settled disputes.

249
00:10:46,395 --> 00:10:49,005
Large charts were placed in the room,

250
00:10:49,055 --> 00:10:51,625
showing the families of New
York and the men in them.

251
00:10:51,675 --> 00:10:55,805
The chart for the Genovese family
alone listed more than a hundred names.

252
00:10:55,855 --> 00:10:58,225
He described the initiation ceremony.

253
00:10:58,275 --> 00:11:00,185
He described the code of silence.

254
00:11:00,235 --> 00:11:02,945
He described murders,
and the reasons for them.

255
00:11:02,995 --> 00:11:04,265
He named names.

256
00:11:04,315 --> 00:11:06,625
Bosses, lieutenants, soldiers.

257
00:11:06,675 --> 00:11:09,165
Men who were still running
businesses in New York,

258
00:11:09,215 --> 00:11:13,065
still eating in the same restaurants,
still living in the same neighborhoods.

259
00:11:13,115 --> 00:11:15,045
Some of them were watching on television.

260
00:11:15,095 --> 00:11:17,945
He also claimed that
Genovese, from prison,

261
00:11:17,995 --> 00:11:21,465
had ordered the deaths of other inmates
who were expected to testify against

262
00:11:21,515 --> 00:11:22,285
him.

263
00:11:22,335 --> 00:11:24,265
Not every answer was clean.

264
00:11:24,315 --> 00:11:25,685
Valachi mixed up dates.

265
00:11:25,735 --> 00:11:29,145
He told some stories differently
from one day to the next.

266
00:11:29,195 --> 00:11:31,875
He sometimes spoke about
events he had only heard about.

267
00:11:31,925 --> 00:11:35,365
Senators pressed him, and
sometimes he grew irritated.

268
00:11:35,415 --> 00:11:37,585
But the core of what
he said held together.

269
00:11:37,635 --> 00:11:40,785
And no one in that room had ever heard
anything like it from the inside.

270
00:11:40,835 --> 00:11:46,185
At one point, Senator McClellan asked
him why he had never simply walked away.

271
00:11:46,235 --> 00:11:49,745
Valachi's answer became one of the
most quoted lines of the hearings.

272
00:11:49,795 --> 00:11:51,925
Once you are in, you can't get out.

273
00:11:51,975 --> 00:11:53,285
They will hunt you.

274
00:11:53,335 --> 00:11:54,525
Across the country,

275
00:11:54,575 --> 00:11:58,285
families watched on black-and-white
television sets as a gangster calmly

276
00:11:58,335 --> 00:12:01,725
described a world most people
had been told did not exist.

277
00:12:01,775 --> 00:12:05,145
The cameras did what Apalachin
had started six years earlier.

278
00:12:05,195 --> 00:12:08,225
The Mafia was no longer
a rumor, or a headline.

279
00:12:08,275 --> 00:12:09,245
It now had a face.

280
00:12:09,295 --> 00:12:10,435
And a voice.

281
00:12:12,526 --> 00:12:15,283
Chapter Seven. What
Changed, And What Didn'T.

282
00:12:15,333 --> 00:12:18,143
Here is the uncomfortable truth
about the Valachi hearings.

283
00:12:18,193 --> 00:12:20,543
They did not put a single boss in prison.

284
00:12:20,593 --> 00:12:24,003
Valachi's testimony never led directly
to the conviction of a major Mafia

285
00:12:24,053 --> 00:12:24,783
leader.

286
00:12:24,833 --> 00:12:26,823
Some of what he said was hearsay.

287
00:12:26,873 --> 00:12:29,263
He was describing events
from decades earlier,

288
00:12:29,313 --> 00:12:32,103
from the point of view
of a soldier, not a boss.

289
00:12:32,153 --> 00:12:35,043
Even inside the Justice
Department, there were doubters.

290
00:12:35,093 --> 00:12:37,363
One senior official, William Hundley,

291
00:12:37,413 --> 00:12:40,903
would later call Cosa Nostra the most
overrated thing since the Communist

292
00:12:40,953 --> 00:12:41,883
Party.

293
00:12:41,933 --> 00:12:43,943
And the timing could not have been worse.

294
00:12:43,993 --> 00:12:46,343
Less than two months
after the hearings began,

295
00:12:46,393 --> 00:12:50,363
on November 22nd, 1963, President John F.

296
00:12:50,413 --> 00:12:52,343
Kennedy was assassinated in Dallas.

297
00:12:52,393 --> 00:12:53,483
His brother Robert,

298
00:12:53,533 --> 00:12:56,683
the man who had pushed the war on
organized crime harder than any attorney

299
00:12:56,733 --> 00:12:59,303
general before him, was devastated.

300
00:12:59,353 --> 00:13:01,163
The campaign lost momentum.

301
00:13:01,213 --> 00:13:04,083
But the hearings changed something
that could not be undone.

302
00:13:04,133 --> 00:13:06,043
They changed what the public believed.

303
00:13:06,093 --> 00:13:08,643
And they changed what
lawmakers were willing to do.

304
00:13:08,693 --> 00:13:12,003
Other witnesses backed up much
of what Valachi described.

305
00:13:12,053 --> 00:13:14,543
Police officials from New York, Detroit,

306
00:13:14,593 --> 00:13:18,383
Chicago, Tampa and Buffalo told the
subcommittee about similar organizations

307
00:13:18,433 --> 00:13:19,883
in their own cities.

308
00:13:19,933 --> 00:13:23,763
In the years that followed, Congress
gave investigators new weapons.

309
00:13:23,813 --> 00:13:28,463
In 1968, a federal law allowed
court-ordered wiretaps in organized crime

310
00:13:28,513 --> 00:13:29,663
cases.

311
00:13:29,713 --> 00:13:34,123
In 1970 came the Racketeer Influenced
and Corrupt Organizations Act.

312
00:13:34,173 --> 00:13:34,643
RICO.

313
00:13:34,693 --> 00:13:38,563
A law that would let prosecutors go
after an entire criminal enterprise,

314
00:13:38,613 --> 00:13:40,863
not just one crime at a time.

315
00:13:40,913 --> 00:13:42,103
In the 1980s,

316
00:13:42,153 --> 00:13:46,023
those tools would help put the heads
of New York's crime families in prison.

317
00:13:46,073 --> 00:13:48,703
Another idea that grew out
of this era was simple:

318
00:13:48,753 --> 00:13:52,903
if the government wanted insiders to
testify, it had to keep them alive.

319
00:13:52,953 --> 00:13:57,873
In the early 1970s, the federal Witness
Security Program was formally created.

320
00:13:57,923 --> 00:14:01,823
Generations of future informants
would disappear into new names and new

321
00:14:01,873 --> 00:14:04,263
cities, the way Valachi never could.

322
00:14:04,313 --> 00:14:07,723
The names Valachi gave to the
New York families stuck, too.

323
00:14:07,773 --> 00:14:11,003
Investigators began referring to them
by the names of their bosses in the

324
00:14:11,053 --> 00:14:16,903
1960s: Genovese, Gambino,
Lucchese, Bonanno and Colombo.

325
00:14:16,953 --> 00:14:18,203
Journalists followed.

326
00:14:18,253 --> 00:14:21,263
Today, they are still
known as the Five Families.

327
00:14:21,313 --> 00:14:23,563
Joe Valachi did not take down the Mafia.

328
00:14:23,613 --> 00:14:25,593
But he helped hand the government the map.

329
00:14:26,349 --> 00:14:28,399
Chapter Eight. The Last Soldier.

330
00:14:28,449 --> 00:14:32,379
After the hearings, Valachi
never walked free again.

331
00:14:32,429 --> 00:14:34,919
He spent his final years
in federal custody,

332
00:14:34,969 --> 00:14:37,359
moved from prison to
prison under protection.

333
00:14:37,409 --> 00:14:39,899
At one point, he tried
to take his own life.

334
00:14:39,949 --> 00:14:40,899
He survived.

335
00:14:40,949 --> 00:14:42,319
In his cell, he wrote.

336
00:14:42,369 --> 00:14:46,799
Hundreds of handwritten pages about his
life, which he called The Real Thing.

337
00:14:46,849 --> 00:14:49,519
The Justice Department first
encouraged the project,

338
00:14:49,569 --> 00:14:51,859
then moved to block its publication.

339
00:14:51,909 --> 00:14:56,319
Eventually, journalist Peter Maas used
those pages and his own interviews with

340
00:14:56,369 --> 00:15:00,839
Valachi to write The Valachi
Papers, published in 1968.

341
00:15:00,889 --> 00:15:04,819
A few years later, it became a
Hollywood film starring Charles Bronson.

342
00:15:04,869 --> 00:15:09,659
As for Vito Genovese, the man whose
kiss started it all, he never got out.

343
00:15:09,709 --> 00:15:15,239
He died in federal custody in 1969,
still officially the boss of his family.

344
00:15:15,289 --> 00:15:18,979
Joe Valachi died on April 3rd, 1971,

345
00:15:19,029 --> 00:15:22,299
at a federal prison in La
Tuna, Texas, near El Paso.

346
00:15:22,349 --> 00:15:24,079
He was sixty-six years old.

347
00:15:24,129 --> 00:15:25,719
The cause was a heart attack.

348
00:15:25,769 --> 00:15:28,059
The bounty on his head
was never collected.

349
00:15:28,109 --> 00:15:29,359
Why did he talk?

350
00:15:29,409 --> 00:15:33,379
Valachi said he wanted to expose an
organization that had ruined his life.

351
00:15:33,429 --> 00:15:35,579
His critics said he wanted
revenge on Genovese,

352
00:15:35,629 --> 00:15:38,359
and protection from a
possible death sentence.

353
00:15:38,409 --> 00:15:40,619
The honest answer is probably all of it.

354
00:15:40,669 --> 00:15:42,439
But think back to 1930.

355
00:15:42,489 --> 00:15:43,979
To that private house.

356
00:15:44,029 --> 00:15:48,059
The gun, the knife, the pin,
the forty men in the room.

357
00:15:48,109 --> 00:15:50,259
And the man who pricked Valachi's finger.

358
00:15:50,309 --> 00:15:51,299
Joe Bonanno.

359
00:15:51,349 --> 00:15:53,399
By the time Valachi testified,

360
00:15:53,449 --> 00:15:57,599
Bonanno was the boss of his own
family, one of the five Valachi named.

361
00:15:57,649 --> 00:16:01,099
And just one year later, in October 1964,

362
00:16:01,149 --> 00:16:05,659
on a rainy night on Park Avenue in New
York, Joe Bonanno would simply vanish.

363
00:16:05,709 --> 00:16:07,419
Kidnapped, his lawyer said.

364
00:16:07,469 --> 00:16:08,659
For nineteen months,

365
00:16:08,709 --> 00:16:11,919
one of the most powerful men
in the American Mafia was gone.

366
00:16:11,969 --> 00:16:14,439
And when he finally walked
back into the world,

367
00:16:14,489 --> 00:16:17,199
he would find a war raging
inside his own family.

368
00:16:17,249 --> 00:16:19,619
That is the next Black Dossier.

369
00:16:19,669 --> 00:16:23,099
If you want to see it, subscribe
and turn on notifications.

370
00:16:23,149 --> 00:16:24,579
And tell me in the comments:

371
00:16:24,629 --> 00:16:27,619
if you had been in Valachi's
place, would you have talked?

372
00:16:27,669 --> 00:16:29,209
I'll see you in the next file.
